      "chunk_id": "text_27",
      "quote": "This review suggests that pain during blind outpatient or office-based endometrial biopsy may be modifiable, but the evidence should be interpreted cautiously. In placebo-, saline-, or placebogel-controlled trials, active pain-control strategies were associated with an average reduction of approximately 1.9 points on a 0-10 intra-procedural pain scale. The finding was directionally stable in leave-one-out analysis and remained similar in the conservative six-trial and balancedbackground-ibuprofen sensitivity analyses. However, the certainty of evidence was low, heterogeneity was substantial, and the prediction interval crossed no effect. ARTICLE IN PRESS The observed average difference is compatible with a clinically relevant reduction for many patients, but clinical importance should not be reduced to a single numeric threshold. In acute pain studies using visual analogue scales, minimum clinically important differences have often been reported around 9 to 13 mm on a 100-mm scale, roughly 0.9 to 1.3 points on a 0-10 scale [30,31]. The pooled mean difference of 1.87 points exceeds these commonly cited thresholds, yet IMMPACT guidance emphasizes that interpretation should also consider baseline pain, patient burden, available alternatives, adverse events, and the risk-benefit profile [32]. In this review, the clinical meaning of the pooled estimate is limited by intervention heterogeneity, low certainty, and incomplete safety reporting.",
      "reason": "The authors interpret the pooled mean difference by comparing it to minimum clinically important differences from other acute pain studies and referencing IMMPACT guidance, thereby placing the results in the context of broader evidence and clinical relevance."
    }
  ],
